  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1121 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nTemperatures flirted close to freezing across the region this \nmorning, with a handful of sites along and east of the Missouri \nRiver dipping just below 32. The official ob at Omaha, however, only \nfell to 33 overnight, extending our streak of above freezing \ntemperatures. If temperatures remain above freezing, as forecast, \nOmaha will likely come very close to, if not break our current \nrecord for latest Fall freeze (November 13 - set in 1915 and 2015). \nIn addition to the cool temperatures, patchy fog and cloud cover \ncould be seen across much of the forecast area this morning. \nSouthwesterly flow brought warmer temperatures into the region, as \nlingering cloud cover and morning fog continued to erode. Highs \ntoday are expected to climb into the 50s to low 60s. \n\nTemperatures will dip back into the upper 30s and low 40s tonight as \na weak northerly wind shift moves through the region. Some patchy \nfog looks possible again, as crossover temperatures are approached. \nLight winds, forecast to drop to 4-10 mph could be just enough to \nkeep dense fog from forming, however overall confidence in \nvisibilities remains low. The best chance for patchy fog currently \nlooks to be along and east of the Missouri River, in addition to \nvalleys and other low-lying areas. \n\nSunday through Tuesday...\n\nAn upper ridge shifting into the north-central US will allow \ntemperatures to warm into the low 60s Sunday afternoon. Slightly \nwarmer temperatures are expected Monday, as the upper ridge \nflattens, and southwesterly low-mid level flow surges ahead of an \napproaching front. Highs Monday afternoon will range from the low \n60s in far northeast Nebraska to the upper 60s and low 70s along and \nsoutheast of I-80. \n\nThe \"cold\" front will push through Monday evening, but will bring \nlittle more than a wind shift to the region. Highs on Tuesday will \ndrop back into the low 60s across the forecast area, which is still \nabout 10 degrees above normal for this time of year. \n\nWednesday and Beyond...\n\nLow level dry air will remain in place keeping us dry through the \nmajority of the coming week. Longer range models hint at a shortwave \ntrough passing through central CONUS Wednesday/Thursday. For now, \nthe majority of ensemble members keep precip associated with that \nsystem just to the east and southeast of the forecast area, however, \nthis far out, it will be worth keeping an eye on. In addition to \nprecipitation chances, this system could also bring us an intrusion \nof cold air, with about 2/3 of GEFS and EPS members dropping the \nmajority of eastern Nebraska and western Iowa below freezing - \ndangerously close to the date of the latest freeze on record.",
